#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# bin/landed — does the remote contain what was reviewed?
#
# THE DEFECT THIS ANSWERS. A merged pull request is not evidence that the branch merged.
# PR #265 reported "Merged" while two commits of its branch — including the only
# behavioural change on it, the fail_closed_models registration for BulkInvoiceRun and
# BulkInvoiceRunRow — were not in the merge and are still not on origin/staging. The merge
# commit's second parent was 37500c8 (a conflict-resolution merge), not the branch tip
# 6e770ae. Nothing looked at origin again after the button was pressed, so nothing noticed.
# The remedy for that gap then repeated the shape one step down: it was merged LOCALLY and
# never pushed, so origin/staging kept a fail-open finance table while the working tree read
# correct. `git status` printed "ahead of origin/staging by 1 commit" the whole time.
# See docs/handoff/tickets/a-merged-pull-request-does-not-mean-the-branch-head-merged.md.
#
# MORE PRINTED TEXT IS NOT THE MECHANISM — the second instance was already visible in
# `git status` and was read by nobody. What was missing is a command with an EXIT CODE, so
# that "it landed" is a claim you can only make by running something that can refuse.
#
# Usage:  bin/landed <branch> [target]        # target defaults to staging
#
# THE ONE MUTATION. This is read-only except for `git fetch origin --prune`, which it always
# runs and which is the only thing it writes (remote-tracking refs). It never pushes, merges,
# checks out, resets or writes a branch ref. A verification tool that changes what it
# verifies is not one.
#
# It operates on the repository containing the CURRENT DIRECTORY — deliberately, unlike
# bin/quality and bin/quality-promote which cd to their own repo root. This is a question
# about refs, not about a tree, and a script hard-wired to one checkout cannot be tested
# against planted repositories.
#
# EXIT CODES — 0 pass, 1 a check failed, 2 could not determine (fetch failed, no such
# branch, not a git repository). 2 IS NOT COLLAPSED INTO 1 ON PURPOSE: "wrong" and "unknown"
# are different answers, and accepting one for the other is the entire defect class this
# exists to close. In particular a failed fetch NEVER exits 0 — a green meaning "I could not
# look" would be the same bug wearing this script's face.
#
# WHICH CHECK DETECTS WHAT. Check 1 detects instance A — a branch holding commits the target
# does not have, whatever the reason. Check 2 detects instance B — a merge that exists only on
# one machine. CHECK 4 DETECTS NOTHING; it explains, and only when origin/<branch> is
# contained in origin/<target>. Do not read a quiet check 4 as evidence of anything: on an
# unmerged branch it deliberately makes no claim, and says so in the output.
#
# THE HONEST LIMIT, in the register of bin/quality's step-15 comment and .githooks/pre-push's
# header. A green here proves exactly two things: that origin/<target> contains every commit
# on origin/<branch>, and — when a merge commit took that head — that it did. It proves
# NOTHING about whether the merge was correct, whether the reviewer read the right diff,
# whether the merged tree passes bin/quality, or whether the branch should have been merged at
# all. It answers one question and no others.
#
# TWO THINGS IT CANNOT SEE. A squash or rebase merge is not a merge to this script — it leaves
# neither a merge commit nor ancestry, so a squash-merged branch reports as not contained,
# exactly like one that never merged. And the merge-message hint printed for an uncontained
# branch is defeated by a rename, an edited merge message or a squash; it is a lead, never a
# verdict, and is labelled as read from the message wherever it appears.
#
# OCTOPUS MERGES ARE READ AS TWO-PARENT MERGES. The second-parent lookup reads field 3 of
# `git rev-list --merges --parents`, so a branch taken as the THIRD or later parent of an
# octopus merge is not found. The EXIT CODE stays correct — the branch is contained, so check 1
# passes and the verdict is still "landed" — but check 4 explains it as "no merge commit found",
# which is the wrong explanation for the right answer. Deliberately not coded around: this
# repository merges one branch at a time (every merge inspected on origin/staging has exactly
# two parents), and widening the field scan would add a loop to the one part of this script whose
# whole recent history is about claiming less, not more. If octopus merges start appearing here,
# that is the moment to revisit it — not before.
#
# NOT WIRED INTO bin/quality, deliberately. That gate is offline and self-contained
# (.githooks/pre-push:3-20), and the failure this catches happens AFTER a merge — a moment
# the per-push hook never observes. A network call in the per-push floor would make every
# push depend on GitHub being reachable, to catch nothing the push itself can cause.
set -uo pipefail

RED=$'\033[31m'; GREEN=$'\033[32m'; YELLOW=$'\033[33m'; BOLD=$'\033[1m'; DIM=$'\033[2m'; OFF=$'\033[0m'

usage() {
    cat <<'USAGE'
bin/landed — does the remote contain what was reviewed?

  Usage:  bin/landed <branch> [target]        # target defaults to staging

Run it after ANY merge. It fetches origin (its only mutation: `git fetch origin --prune`)
and then answers, from refs alone:

  1. does origin/<target> contain every commit on origin/<branch>?
  2. is your local <target> ahead of origin — i.e. is a merge sitting unpushed?
  3. is your local <target> behind origin?                       (information, not a failure)
  4. WHEN the branch is contained: did a merge take the head origin/<branch> now points at?

Checks 1 and 2 are the detectors. CHECK 4 IS AN EXPLAINER: it makes no claim about a branch
that is not contained in the target, because git records no branch identity in the DAG and a
merged-then-advanced branch is topologically identical to one whose fork point happens to be
a merge parent. For an uncontained branch it may print a hint read from a merge MESSAGE; that
never affects the exit code.

It never pushes, merges, checks out, resets or writes a branch ref, and it never calls `gh`.

Exit codes:  0 all checks pass · 1 a check failed · 2 could not determine.
A failed fetch exits 2, never 0 — a green that means "I could not look" is the bug itself.

A green proves origin/<target> contains origin/<branch>, and where a merge commit took that
head, that it did. Nothing about whether the merge was correct or the merged tree is green.
USAGE
}

case "${1:-}" in
    -h|--help|help) usage; exit 0 ;;
esac

BRANCH="${1:-}"
TARGET="${2:-staging}"

if [ -z "$BRANCH" ]; then
    usage >&2
    printf '\n%s✗ no branch given%s\n' "$RED" "$OFF" >&2
    exit 2
fi

# Tolerate `bin/landed origin/feat/x` — the remote-tracking name is what a reader has in
# front of them, and refusing it teaches nothing.
BRANCH="${BRANCH#origin/}"
TARGET="${TARGET#origin/}"

if ! git rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '%s✗ not a git repository — cannot determine anything%s\n' "$RED" "$OFF" >&2
    exit 2
fi

BR_REF="refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH"
TG_REF="refs/remotes/origin/$TARGET"
LOCAL_TG="refs/heads/$TARGET"

printf '%s%sLANDED?%s  %s → %s\n\n' "$BOLD" "$YELLOW" "$OFF" "$BRANCH" "$TARGET"

# --- 0a. the graph must be whole ---------------------------------------------
# A SHALLOW CLONE CANNOT ANSWER EITHER QUESTION, and answers both anyway if allowed to. The
# merge sits below the graft, so `git merge-base --is-ancestor` cannot see containment and a
# LANDED branch reports NOT landed; the outstanding count is computed over a truncated graph
# and is wrong. A false red and a wrong number are still answers this script cannot give, so
# it says it cannot give them. Anything other than a clean `true`/`false` is also unknown —
# an unexpected value is not evidence of a whole graph.
SHALLOW="$(git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository 2>/dev/null || true)"
case "$SHALLOW" in
    false) ;;
    true)
        printf '%s✗ this is a SHALLOW clone — the merge may sit below the graft%s\n' "$RED" "$OFF"
        printf '%s  containment and the outstanding count are both unanswerable here. Unshallow first:%s\n' "$DIM" "$OFF"
        printf '%s  git fetch --unshallow%s\n' "$DIM" "$OFF"
        exit 2 ;;
    *)
        printf '%s✗ could not determine whether this clone is shallow (got [%s])%s\n' "$RED" "$SHALLOW" "$OFF"
        exit 2 ;;
esac

# --- 0b. fetch ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The only write this script performs.
#
# THE TWO REFS ARE NAMED, and that is the whole point of this block rather than a detail of
# it. A bare `git fetch --prune origin` honours remote.origin.fetch — and a `--single-branch`
# or `--depth` clone leaves that refspec covering ONE branch. The fetch then succeeds, updates
# nothing for the other ref, and the script printed a ✓ and compared a ref that had not moved:
# measured as `✓ landed`, EXIT 0, over a branch carrying two commits the target did not have.
# A FALSE GREEN IN A VERIFICATION TOOL IS WORSE THAN NO TOOL — it converts "I did not check"
# into "I checked". Naming the refspec makes freshness a property of THIS fetch instead of a
# property of the clone's configuration.
#
# --prune consequently operates only WITHIN this refspec, which is intentional: pruning
# unrelated remote-tracking refs was never this script's business, and a verification tool
# should not delete refs it is not talking about.
#
# TWO UNKNOWNS, TWO MESSAGES. A fetch that fails because the ref is absent on origin is a
# different thing from a fetch that fails because origin is unreachable, and collapsing them
# would leave a reader retrying the network over a typo.
if ! fetch_out="$(git fetch --prune origin \
    "+refs/heads/$BRANCH:refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH" \
    "+refs/heads/$TARGET:refs/remotes/origin/$TARGET" 2>&1)"; then

    case "$fetch_out" in
        *"couldn't find remote ref"*|*"could not find remote ref"*)
            printf '%s✗ no such branch on origin — cannot determine whether it landed%s\n' "$RED" "$OFF"
            printf '%s  asked for refs/heads/%s and refs/heads/%s%s\n' "$DIM" "$BRANCH" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
            printf '%s  (a branch deleted at merge is indistinguishable from one that never existed)%s\n' "$DIM" "$OFF"
            ;;
        *)
            printf '%s✗ could not reach origin — this check is meaningless offline%s\n' "$RED" "$OFF"
            ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$fetch_out" ] && printf '%s%s%s\n' "$DIM" "$fetch_out" "$OFF"
    exit 2
fi
printf '%s✓%s fetched origin/%s and origin/%s by name (--prune, within that refspec)\n' \
    "$GREEN" "$OFF" "$BRANCH" "$TARGET"

# Belt and braces: a fetch can succeed and still leave a ref unresolvable if the remote served
# nothing for it. These two guards are what the checks below stand on.
if ! git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$BR_REF" >/dev/null; then
    printf '%s✗ no such branch origin/%s — cannot determine whether it landed%s\n' "$RED" "$BRANCH" "$OFF"
    printf '%s  (a branch deleted at merge is indistinguishable from one that never existed)%s\n' "$DIM" "$OFF"
    exit 2
fi
if ! git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$TG_REF" >/dev/null; then
    printf '%s✗ no such branch origin/%s — cannot determine anything%s\n' "$RED" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
    exit 2
fi

BR_SHA="$(git rev-parse "$BR_REF")"
TG_SHA="$(git rev-parse "$TG_REF")"
printf '%s  origin/%s = %s · origin/%s = %s%s\n\n' "$DIM" "$BRANCH" "${BR_SHA:0:8}" "$TARGET" "${TG_SHA:0:8}" "$OFF"

FAILED=0
CONTAINED_NO_MERGE=0

# --- 1. the branch may not hold commits the target lacks ---------------------
# This is instance A of the ticket, and it also catches instance B from the other side.
OUTSTANDING="$(git rev-list --count "$TG_REF..$BR_REF")"
if [ "$OUTSTANDING" != "0" ]; then
    FAILED=$((FAILED + 1))
    printf '%s✗ origin/%s has %s commit(s) that origin/%s does not have%s\n' \
        "$RED" "$BRANCH" "$OUTSTANDING" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
    git log --oneline "$TG_REF..$BR_REF" | sed 's/^/    /'
else
    printf '%s✓%s origin/%s contains every commit on origin/%s\n' "$GREEN" "$OFF" "$TARGET" "$BRANCH"
fi

# --- 2. the LOCAL target may not be ahead of origin --------------------------
# Instance B: the merge that closes a gap, sitting on one machine. Only meaningful when a
# local branch of that name exists — a clone that never checked out staging has nothing to
# be ahead of.
if git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$LOCAL_TG" >/dev/null; then
    AHEAD="$(git rev-list --count "$TG_REF..$LOCAL_TG")"
    if [ "$AHEAD" != "0" ]; then
        FAILED=$((FAILED + 1))
        printf '%s✗ your local %s has %s commit(s) that are not on origin%s\n' "$RED" "$TARGET" "$AHEAD" "$OFF"
        git log --oneline "$TG_REF..$LOCAL_TG" | sed 's/^/    /'
        printf '%s  a merge nobody else can see has not landed. Push %s.%s\n' "$DIM" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
    else
        printf '%s✓%s your local %s is not ahead of origin\n' "$GREEN" "$OFF" "$TARGET"
    fi

    # --- 3. behind is INFORMATION, not a failure -----------------------------
    # Being behind is the normal state of any checkout between pulls. Being ahead is the
    # defect. Reporting the two the same way would train the reader to ignore both.
    BEHIND="$(git rev-list --count "$LOCAL_TG..$TG_REF")"
    if [ "$BEHIND" != "0" ]; then
        printf '%sℹ your local %s is %s commit(s) behind origin — normal; `git pull --ff-only` when you need them%s\n' \
            "$YELLOW" "$TARGET" "$BEHIND" "$OFF"
    fi
else
    printf '%sℹ no local %s branch — the ahead/behind checks do not apply%s\n' "$YELLOW" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
fi

# --- 4. WHAT THE MERGE TOOK — an explainer, not a detector -------------------
# THIS CHECK CANNOT DETECT A STALE MERGE HEAD, and its first version claimed to. Instance A
# (a branch merged at an earlier sha, then advanced) and a branch cut from a commit that
# later became the second parent of a reconciliation merge are THE SAME TOPOLOGY: in both, a
# merge on the target has as its second parent a commit that is an ancestor of the branch and
# is not the branch head. Git records no branch identity in the DAG, so no ancestry test
# separates them — a matcher sharpened until it passed one would fail the other.
#
# That is measured, not reasoned about. The first version matched the merge whose second
# parent equalled merge-base(target, branch); pointed at this repository it reported
# `feat/landed-check` — never merged — as having been "merged" at its own fork point, because
# a `git pull` reconciliation merge on staging had that fork point as its second parent.
# Fifteen mutations against fixtures had not found it; one run against a real repository did.
# Raw output and derivation: docs/handoff/reports/feat-landed-check.md § 7.
#
# So the topological claim is made ONLY when origin/<branch> is CONTAINED in origin/<target> —
# a fact about the graph, not a guess about intent. When it is not contained, check 4 asserts
# nothing and adds nothing to the failure count. An unmerged branch is check 1's business, and
# check 1 has already named the outstanding commits.
if git merge-base --is-ancestor "$BR_REF" "$TG_REF" 2>/dev/null; then
    # Contained. The merge that took this branch, if there is one, is the merge on the target
    # whose SECOND PARENT IS THE BRANCH HEAD. Containment plus that equality is reliable in a
    # way the merge-base form was not: the branch head is inside the target AND is what a
    # merge took, so there is nothing else the merge could have taken instead.
    MERGE="$(git rev-list --merges --parents "$TG_REF" | awk -v h="$BR_SHA" '$3 == h { print $1; exit }')"

    if [ -n "$MERGE" ]; then
        # The PR number, when there is one, is READ FROM THE MERGE COMMIT'S OWN MESSAGE and
        # labelled as such. `gh` is deliberately not consulted: it can be absent,
        # unauthenticated or rate-limited, and every fact here is answerable from refs.
        SUBJECT="$(git log -1 --format=%s "$MERGE")"
        printf '%s✓%s the merge on origin/%s took the head origin/%s points at (%s)\n' \
            "$GREEN" "$OFF" "$TARGET" "$BRANCH" "${BR_SHA:0:8}"
        printf '%s  merge %s — "%s" (subject read from the merge commit)%s\n' \
            "$DIM" "${MERGE:0:8}" "$SUBJECT" "$OFF"
    else
        # Contained with no merge commit: a fast-forward, or the branch merged the target back
        # into itself after its own PR closed. Neither is the defect and neither is a
        # second-parent match, so it is reported as its own outcome.
        CONTAINED_NO_MERGE=1
        printf '%sℹ no merge commit of %s found on origin/%s, but origin/%s is contained in origin/%s%s\n' \
            "$YELLOW" "$BRANCH" "$TARGET" "$BRANCH" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
        printf '%s  fast-forward, or the branch merged %s back in after its own merge. Check 1 is the signal here.%s\n' \
            "$DIM" "$TARGET" "$OFF"
    fi
else
    printf '%sℹ origin/%s is not contained in origin/%s — no merge-head claim is made. Check 1 above is the signal.%s\n' \
        "$YELLOW" "$BRANCH" "$TARGET" "$OFF"

    # THE DIAGNOSIS, DEMOTED TO A HINT. "PR merged X, branch head is Y" is the useful sentence
    # for instance A, and it is NOT derivable from topology — only from what a merge commit
    # SAYS about itself. So it is read from the message, printed as a hint, labelled as read
    # from the message, and it never touches the failure count. Same treatment and same reason
    # as the PR number above.
    #
    # A renamed branch, an edited merge message, or a squash defeats it entirely. It is a lead
    # for a human, never a verdict.
    # `*"/$BRANCH"` WOULD SPAN SLASHES. A shell glob's `*` matches `/` freely, so
    # `bin/landed x` matched `… from owner/feat/x` and attributed another branch's merge. The
    # remainder after " from " must be exactly "<owner>/$BRANCH" with a SLASH-FREE owner, so
    # the branch component is compared whole rather than as a suffix.
    HINT=""
    while IFS=' ' read -r msha msubject; do
        case "$msubject" in
            "Merge pull request #"*" from "*)
                ref="${msubject#* from }"
                owner="${ref%%/*}"
                case "$owner" in */*) continue ;; esac
                [ "$owner/$BRANCH" = "$ref" ] && { HINT="$msha"; break; }
                ;;
            "Merge branch '$BRANCH'"|"Merge branch '$BRANCH' "*) HINT="$msha"; break ;;
        esac
    done < <(git log --merges --format='%H %s' "$TG_REF")

    if [ -n "$HINT" ]; then
        HINT_P2="$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$HINT^2" || true)"
        if [ -n "$HINT_P2" ]; then
            HINT_GAP="$(git rev-list --count "$HINT_P2..$BR_REF" 2>/dev/null || echo '?')"
            printf '%s  hint, READ FROM A MERGE MESSAGE and not from the graph: merge %s says it\n' "$DIM" "${HINT:0:8}"
            printf '  merged this branch and took %s; origin/%s is now %s, %s commit(s) beyond that.%s\n' \
                "${HINT_P2:0:8}" "$BRANCH" "${BR_SHA:0:8}" "$HINT_GAP" "$OFF"
            printf '%s  merge %s — "%s"%s\n' "$DIM" "${HINT:0:8}" "$(git log -1 --format=%s "$HINT")" "$OFF"
            printf '%s  A hint, not a verdict: a rename, an edited message or a squash defeats it.%s\n' "$DIM" "$OFF"
        fi
    fi
fi

# --- verdict -----------------------------------------------------------------
echo
if [ "$FAILED" -eq 0 ]; then
    # TWO DIFFERENT GREENS, AND THEY MUST NOT SHARE A SENTENCE. Where there is no merge commit
    # there is no "reviewed head" to have been taken, and a verdict that claimed one anyway would
    # be asserting more than the checks above established — which is the reporting habit the ticket
    # this script closes is about.
    if [ "$CONTAINED_NO_MERGE" -eq 1 ]; then
        printf '%s✓ landed%s — origin/%s contains origin/%s, with no merge commit to check.\n' \
            "$GREEN" "$OFF" "$TARGET" "$BRANCH"
    else
        printf '%s✓ landed%s — origin/%s contains origin/%s, and the merge took the reviewed head.\n' \
            "$GREEN" "$OFF" "$TARGET" "$BRANCH"
    fi
    printf '%sThis says nothing about whether the merge was correct or the merged tree is green.%s\n' "$DIM" "$OFF"
    exit 0
fi

printf '%s✗ NOT landed%s (%d check(s) failed) — do not report %s as merged.\n' "$RED" "$OFF" "$FAILED" "$BRANCH"
exit 1
